-- Links Neldner et al. (2026), "Children and adults across 15 countries believe in
-- human uniqueness of mind" (DOI 10.1016/j.jenvp.2025.102861,
-- OE-LITERATURE-neldner-etal-2026-human-uniqueness-of-mind) to both synthetic LPMs.
--
-- Requested directly by Dustin Eirdosh (co-author of this paper, alongside Susan
-- Hanisch) to inform the costs/benefits of using cognitive systems (animal minds,
-- human minds, computational/distributed cognition) as a domain example in an
-- integrated (ICR/CFCR) vs. dichotomized (DCR) way. The LiteratureBase record is on
-- an open PR (openevo-ccs/literaturebase#12), not yet merged to literaturebase's own
-- main -- literaturebase_snapshot is upserted directly in this migration (rather than
-- via scripts/sync_literaturebase_snapshot.mjs, which reads a sibling checkout's
-- working tree) so this is a self-contained, re-runnable migration that does not
-- depend on that PR's merge state. A future sync run will simply overwrite this row
-- with the same data once literaturebase#12 lands.
--
-- Neither project's theory is this paper's real subject -- it is a cross-cultural
-- developmental-psychology finding about folk belief, not a learning-progression or
-- restructuration paper -- so both notes below state its relevance plainly rather
-- than forcing a "grounds this theory" frame (163's own documented convention for
-- exactly this case). Confirmed live 2026-10-23: no existing literature_references
-- row for this literaturebase_id on either project.

SET search_path = public, extensions;

