  '{"$schema": "../schema/literature-record.schema.json", "id": "OE-LITERATURE-neldner-etal-2026-human-uniqueness-of-mind", "slug": "neldner-etal-2026-human-uniqueness-of-mind", "citation": {"title": "Children and adults across 15 countries believe in human uniqueness of mind: a cross-cultural investigation of cross-species mind perception", "year": 2026, "authors": ["Neldner, Karri", "Maurits, Luke", "Junker, Magie", "Abbas, Lara", "Abbas, Nayrouz", "Abis, Arianna", "Amici, Federica", "Arroyo-Garcia, Bernardo", "Asghari, Negar", "Barragán Pardo, Giovanna", "Zhang, Zhen", "Peña Chumacero, Junior", "Dzabatou, Ardain", "Eirdosh, Dustin", "Hanisch, Susan", "Herrnsdorf, Tom", "Hovehne, Tom", "Junker, Alicia", "Kanngiesser, Patricia", "Villa Larens, Felipe", "Mahmoud, Safaa", "Masaquiza, Sandra", "Iino, Masato", "Maulany, Risma Illa", "Meng, Tongtong", "Mutlu, Kardelen", "Ngakan, Putu Oka", "Peközer, Ebru", "Petrović, Ljubica", "Rehatalanit, Mirella Christy", "Piscayanti, Kadek Sonia", "Pope-Caldwell, Sarah", "Sandoval Sernaque, Maria Inés", "Shishala, Dennis", "Sportelli, Doriana", "Stengelin, Roman", "Stodulka, Thomas", "Striegler, Blanca", "Sun, Wanting", "Sunderarajan, Jahnavi", "Tempelmann, Sebastian", "Thajib, Ferdiansyah", "Thiede, Noemi", "Tjizao, Disney", "Useb, Linus", "Woidich, Lena", "Weyrowitz, Janina", "Haun, Daniel", "Liebal, Katja"], "venue": "Journal of Environmental Psychology", "publisher": "Elsevier BV", "volume": "109", "pages": "102861", "doi": "10.1016/j.jenvp.2025.102861", "open_access_url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0272494425003445"}, "type": "journal-article", "license": "cc-by-4.0", "domains": ["Comparative Psychology", "Developmental Psychology", "Cross-Cultural Psychology", "Cognitive Science", "Environmental Psychology"], "plain_language_summary": {"text": "A very large, 15-country study asked children (age 4 and up) and adults whether animals have thoughts and feelings, and whether those thoughts and feelings work the same way human thoughts and feelings do. Across every community tested -- rural and urban, on five continents -- most people happily agreed that animals do think and feel something. But almost everyone, including young children, drew a firm line at calling any of it \"human-like\": people across all 15 countries denied that an animal''s thoughts resemble a human''s own. This belief in a special, human-only kind of thinking showed up by early childhood and stayed just as strong in adults, in every culture studied. Beliefs about animal feelings (rather than thoughts) varied more from place to place. The authors call this consistent pattern \"human mental exceptionalism\" and discuss what it means for how people treat and make conservation decisions about other animals.", "written_by": "llm", "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "date": "2026-10-23", "reviewed_by_expert": false}, "related": [{"id": "OE-LITERATURE-call-tomasello-2008-chimpanzee-theory-of-mind-30-years-later", "relation": "related", "note": "Opposite side of the same question. Call & Tomasello review what great apes actually understand about others'' minds (the scientific evidence for animal theory-of-mind competence); this paper instead measures what ordinary people of all ages and cultures believe about animal minds, independent of the underlying science. The two records together separate the \"is it true\" question from the \"do people believe it\" question that OpenEvo''s Cross-Species Comparisons and Our Mind content anchors both have to address."}, {"id": "OE-LITERATURE-wimmer-perner-1983-beliefs-about-beliefs", "relation": "related", "note": "Wimmer & Perner established that human theory-of-mind reasoning (understanding another mind can hold a false belief) develops in early childhood via a specific task design. This paper finds that an early, stable, cross-cultural belief *about* the uniqueness of human minds relative to animal minds is already in place by the same early-childhood window the false-belief literature studies, in effect bounding the Wimmer & Perner developmental story by a parallel belief about who minds like this belong to."}], "relevant_to_theory": [], "status": "accepted", "provenance": {"extracted_by": "llm", "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "added_by": "dustin-eirdosh", "date": "2026-10-23", "review_status": "author-draft", "version": "1.0.0", "contributor_notes": "Requested directly by Dustin Eirdosh (co-author of this paper, alongside Susan Hanisch) for ingestion into LiteratureBase and consideration for the OpenLPM bio-core-lpm-synth vs. interdisciplinary-lpm-synth comparative study. ScienceDirect blocked direct automated fetching of the article page (HTTP 403); full abstract text was instead cross-verified against two independent institutional-repository mirrors (University of Bern PH Bern repository, phrepo.phbern.ch/7981, and University of Plymouth PEARL repository, pearl.plymouth.ac.uk/psy-research/1229), which agreed verbatim on the abstract text and both confirmed CC BY 4.0 licensing. DOI and full 48-author list verified live against Crossref (api.crossref.org/works/10.1016/j.jenvp.2025.102861). This record reflects the abstract and bibliographic metadata only -- the full paper''s methods/results sections (e.g. the specific Bayesian model structure, per-country breakdowns, and the discussion of conservation-ethics implications) have not been read in full text and should be checked before relying on any finer-grained claim beyond what this record''s plain_language_summary states."}}'::jsonb
